same mind; like-minded. ''Bp. Hall'', Remains, p. 82.

'''alim''' (ä′lēm), ''n.'' [Ar. ''‘ālim'', ''‘ālīm'', learned, < ''‘alama'', know. Cf. ''alem'', ''almah''.] Among Mohammedans, a learned man; a religious teacher, such as an imâm, a mufti, etc.
{{smaller block|The calling of an ''Alim'' is no longer worth much in Egypt.
{{right|''R. F. Burton'', El-Medinah, p. 93.}}}}

'''Alima''' (al′i-mạ̈), ''n.'' [NL., for ''Halima'', < Gr. ἅλιμος, of the sea.] A spurious genus of crustaceans, representing a stage of stomatopodous crustaceans, for which the term is still in use.
{{smaller block|In the ''Alima'' type of development [of ''Stomatopoda''], it seems that the young leaves the egg in nearly the ''Alima'' form, and in the youngest stage known the six appendages, eight to thirteen, are absent, although three of the corresponding segments of the body are developed.
{{right|''Stand. Nat. Hist.'', II. 66.}}}}

'''aliment''' (al′i-mẹnt), ''n.'' [< late ME. ''aliment'', < F. ''aliment'', < L. ''alimentum'', food, < ''alere'', nourish, = Goth. ''alan'', be nourished, ''aljan'', nourish, fatten, = Icel. ''ala'', beget, bear, nourish, support; cf. ''alie''<sup>1</sup>, and ''alt'', ''all'', and ''old''.] '''1.''' That which nourishes or sustains; food; nutriment; sustenance; support, whether literal or figurative.
{{smaller block|Those elevated meditations which are the proper ''aliment'' of noble souls. {{right|''Irving'', Sketch-Book, p. 30.}}}}
'''2.''' In ''Scots law'', the sum paid for support to any one entitled to claim it, as the dole given to a pauper by his parish.
{{smaller block|The ''aliment'' was appointed to continue till the majority or marriage of the daughters. {{right|''Erskine'', Institutes.}}}}

